    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 090521\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1020 PM MST Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures will prevail through the rest of the \nweek resulting in widespread moderate Heat Risk and areas of \nmajor Heat Risk.\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings will be in effect for portions of the lower \ndeserts through the end of the week.\n\n- Thunderstorm coverage is expected to increase during the latter \nhalf of the week across Eastern and Central Arizona, with slight \nchances becoming introduced for the South Central Arizona lower \ndeserts by Thursday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nNot much has changed in the current upper atmosphere conditions over \nthe past 24 hours with the region remaining between a large \ntroughing feature over much of Western CONUS, and an expansive \nridging over the Southern Plains and Mississippi River Valley \nregion. Mid to high level clouds continue to stream northeastwards \nthrough the late morning hours due to southwesterly flow aloft. \nHowever the flow will begin shifting out of the southeast as the \nsubtropical high moves westwards towards the Four Corners region \nstarting this afternoon. The subtropical high is expected to peak \nWednesday, with H5 heights quickly building to 596-597 dam, near to \nat NAEFS climatological max for this time of year. This will help \ndrive afternoon high temperatures across the lower deserts around 5F \nabove normal for this time of year. Additionally, while \nsouthwesterly flow usually advects drier air into the region, \nPacific tropical moisture was able to be pulled in keeping good \nmoisture in the mid to upper atmosphere. This has lead to overnight \ncloud coverage hindering radiative cooling and PWATs have remained \nbetween 130-150% of normal, with morning dew points consistently \nreading in the mid 50s to low 60s. Due to these factors the \novernight low temperatures are in the mid to upper 80s, or near 7F-\n10F above normal, and providing very little relief in the heat \nthroughout this week. Major HeatRisk has resulted across a large \nportion of the lower deserts and an Extreme Heat Warning is in \neffect for the Phoenix Metro, portions of South-Central and \nSouthwest AZ including the Yuma area, and portions of Imperial \nCounty including the Imperial Valley. Extreme Heat Warnings expand \nslightly further north Wednesday along the Lower CO River Valley and \nsurrounding communities. \n\nAs mentioned earlier the subtropical high will begin shifting winds \naloft out of the southeast starting today and will continue through \nthe week, with daily gulf surges likely. A notable increase to 1000- \n700 mb mixing ratios will occur Wednesday night quickly increasing \nto 10-12 g/kg across SC AZ and even into the lower western deserts \nstarting wednesday. Unfortunately, with the subtropical high \nexpected to be directly over head, strong anticyclonic subsidence \nstorms will struggle to develop in the low deserts, although not \nimpossible. HREF models continue to show between a 30-50% chance of \nstorms developing in southeastern AZ, and chances expand northwards \ninto SC AZ, even into southern Pinal County by Late Wednesday, but \nwith the ridge overhead it will remain conditional.  \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...    \nLate this week is shaping up to be a more active period for monsoon \nthunderstorms across at least South-Central AZ. By Thursday, \nanticyclonic subsidence will begin to diminish as the subtropical \nhigh weakens overhead, and abundant low level moisture will remain \nin place through the rest of the week and linger into the weekend. \nLatest NBM guidance seems to favor Friday with the best chances for \nlower desert thunderstorms, which makes sense given the weaker \nsubsidence aloft and plenty of moisture and instability to support \nnumerous thunderstorms over the high terrain by the afternoon, \nsending outflows into the lower deserts and triggering additional \nthunderstorms by the evening. GFS bufr soundings indicate a good \nbalance between CAPE and DCAPE Thursday evening, with values between \n1000-1500 J/kg, which is typically a good indicator for severe \nweather potential over the Valley. However, CIN may be a bit \nstronger than preferred, and the development of thunderstorms that \ncan utilize this environment will be entirely dependent on the \npresence and timing of a sufficient lifting mechanism (i.e., \nthunderstorm outflow boundary). This conditional threat continues \nFriday, but with perhaps lower DCAPE and shallower inverted-V \nsoundings. Strong thunderstorm winds, dense blowing dust, locally \nheavy rainfall resulting in instances of flash flooding, and \nfrequent lightning will all be on the table for thunderstorm hazards \nlater this week.\n\nThe forecast for this weekend into early next week is much less \ncertain, however, a few more details are coming into focus tonight \ncompared to the guidance available in previous forecast updates. \nEnsembles are in better agreement that mean troughing will establish \nover the Northwest US, pushing the northern stream and likely a \nseries of upper lows and their attendant jet streaks as far south as \nthe Great Basin. WPC cluster analysis reveals discrepancies on how \nfar south the mean troughing will establish and how strongly \nnegative the midlevel height anomalies will become over the \nNorthwest US. The current consensus is for flow aloft to shift south \nto southwesterly as a result, meaning the more typical monsoonal \npattern which briefly sets up this week will be short-lived, \nbecoming something more akin to a transition season setup. This does \nnot, however, mean that thunderstorm chances leave the forecast. \nDrying will begin to occur from west to east, but low level moisture \nwill not be scoured out from Southern AZ. Thunderstorms will likely \ncontinue to favor development over prominent terrain features, and \nlower desert storm chances will be dependent on outflows or a nearly \nuncapped environment. As for now, NBM/WPC PoPs have come in lower \nthis weekend compared to Thu/Fri, and timing of the better chances \nseems a bit unrealistic, reflecting the high uncertainty and \nnebulous forcing mechanisms. Regardless, the main message for this \nweekend into early next week will be continued thunderstorm chances \nover at least South-Central AZ higher terrain areas, but likely a \ndecrease in activity from Thu/Fri due to the pattern shift and until \nsoutheasterly flow returns. \n\nWith the subtropical high diminished and then displaced by Northwest \nUS troughing this weekend, temperatures will follow a downward \ntrend, attaining near normal values likely by Saturday or Sunday, \nending the extreme heat conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0520Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major weather concerns will exist through Wednesday night under \noccasional mid/high cigs, patchy virga, and isold SHRA. Confidence \nin wind forecast are somewhat lowered tonight as a SW heading may \nredevelop due to a Gulf surge before weakening again and becoming \neast/variable closer to sunrise. Directions should switch back to \nW/SW by noon Wednesday with limited afternoon gusts. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather concerns will exist through Wednesday night under \noccasional mid/high cigs and patchy virga/SHRA. Confidence in wind \ntrends is only moderate with a tendency for a NW direction to become \nmore southerly during the day Wednesday. Extended periods of nearly \ncalm conditions may be common during this transition. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAbove normal temperatures will prevail through the end of the work \nweek, then temperatures are likely to decrease closer to normal over \nthe weekend. Afternoon minimum humidities will generally range \nbetween 18-30% areawide through much of the week following fair to \ngood overnight recoveries between 35-70%. Fairly typical diurnal \nwinds with occasional afternoon upslope breeziness can be expected \nthrough Thursday, followed by an increase in southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds Friday into this weekend. Rain and thunderstorm \nchances will remain low and mostly limited to the eastern Arizona \nhigh terrain through midweek before slight chances become introduced \nto the lower deserts of at least the eastern districts during the \nlatter half of the week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ532.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Thursday for AZZ531-535>544-\n     546-548>551-559.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM Wednesday to 8 PM MST Friday for \n     AZZ530-533.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-566-567.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM Wednesday to 8 PM PDT Friday for \n     CAZ569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Whittock \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock\n",
